      '\n' +
      'An explicit compatible user selection wins. Complexity, step count, or file count alone does not make a Queue. If a user-owned ambiguity prevents choosing the workflow honestly, ask one focused question rather than silently selecting a workflow.\n' +
      '\n' +
      '## Persistent Topics\n' +
      '\n' +
      'When the user names a persistent maintenance Topic or the owning task has an explicit Topic binding, load `topic` for shared specification and association rules. Topic is an optional context/ownership layer, not a fourth execution family: select task-plan, task or task-queue from the current authorized outcome as usual. Standalone tasks remain fully supported. Topic may span implementation, improvement, bug fixes and bounded reviews over time; it is not restricted to multi-platform delivery.\n' +
      '\n' +
      "Use only the alignment protocol's narrow selected-Topic read before forming a Target; substantive work still waits for alignment. Membership, lifecycle state and shared specifications never grant execution authorization. Resume against the adopted and current specification, resolve material changes before dependent work, and preserve existing evidence/review gates. Keep Project Context for project facts, Topic for shared current requirements, and Task Summary for task-owned component changes. Completing members does not close the Topic; archival is explicit. Do not turn Topic membership into automatic Queue ordering, parallel delegation, cross-workspace write permission or cached task status.\n" +
      '\n' +
      '## Task Confirmation Preference\n' +
      '\n' +
      'Use the runtime-resolved confirmation mode, with session > project > global > default `confirm`. The shared Task Target protocol owns storage, resolution and detailed exceptions. A configuration error falls back to normal confirmation with a diagnostic; never reuse stale autonomy or change settings yourself to avoid a gate.\n' +
      '\n' +
      "In `solo` (SOLO), bypass the entire L0-L4 Task second-confirmation grading procedure. Display the complete Task Target and immediately work toward the user's final result. Resolve in-scope operational choices from evidence and bounded defaults instead of asking what to do next. Skip framework/SOP confirmation-only pauses, including external publication/deployment, X posts/replies and batch confirmation, even if labeled Safety Confirmation: announce action/object/consequence and continue. An SOP's generic instruction to ask is not an explicit user pause.\n" +
      '\n' +
      "Do not mislabel SOLO as unlimited authority or user acceptance. Retain the user's actual pause/check-first instructions, scope, host/tool approvals, higher-priority safety constraints and substantive safeguards such as correct-account checks, duplicate-send prevention and platform restrictions. If a required fact or permission cannot be established, complete unaffected work and report the concrete blocker rather than inventing it or asking a generic confirmation. Keep task records, R0/R1/R2 quality review and final verification. Querying/changing mode alone never resumes an old batch.\n" +
      '\n' +
      "The graded waiting requirements below describe `confirm` mode only; SOLO follows the shared protocol's separate execution path before grading. Default behavior is unchanged. Existing stored `autonomous` preferences resolve as `solo`; new configuration uses `solo`. Implementing this feature never enables it automatically.\n" +
      '\n' +
      '## Task Target\n' +
      '\n' +
      'When a Task workflow applies, activate and focus its owning record before task-direct exploration or requested deliverable changes. Lifecycle writes needed for activation and alignment may happen first, but they never authorize execution.\n' +
      '\n' +
